When you delete a note in your SvelteKit application and the UI does not update, it's likely because the state management for the list of notes is not being properly handled or reloaded after the deletion action.

Here’s a step-by-step guide to help you resolve this issue:

### 1. Ensure State Management

First, make sure that the state of the notes is managed correctly. If you're using Svelte's built-in store system (like `writable`), ensure that the store is being updated when a note is deleted.

#### Example Using Svelte Store

```svelte
<script>
  import { writable } from 'svelte/store';
  let notes = writable([]);

  // Function to fetch notes from API
  async function loadNotes() {
    const response = await fetch('/api/notes');
    const data = await response.json();
    notes.set(data);
  }

  // Load notes initially
  loadNotes();

  // Function to delete a note
  async function deleteNote(id) {
    await fetch(`/api/notes/${id}`, { method: 'DELETE' });
    // Update the store by filtering out the deleted note
    notes.update(notes => notes.filter(note => note.id !== id));
  }
</script>

<ul>
  {#each $notes as note}
    <li>
      {note.title} - {note.content}
      <button on:click={() => deleteNote(note.id)}>Delete</button>
    </li>
  {/each}
</ul>
```

### 2. Use Form Actions Correctly

If you're using form actions to handle deletions, ensure that the form submission triggers a reload of the notes list.

#### Example Using Form Action

```svelte
<script>
  import { page } from '$app/stores';
  import { onDestroy } from 'svelte';

  let notes;

  // Fetch notes on page load
  async function loadNotes() {
    const response = await fetch('/api/notes');
    notes = await response.json();
  }

  // Delete note via form action
  async function deleteNote(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    const formData = new FormData(event.target);
    const id = formData.get('id');

    await fetch(`/api/notes/${id}`, { method: 'DELETE' });

    // Reload notes after deletion
    await loadNotes();
  }

  // Load notes initially
  loadNotes();

  // Optionally, listen for changes in URL parameters if needed
  onDestroy(() => {
    // Cleanup logic if necessary
  });
</script>

<form method="POST" on:submit={deleteNote}>
  <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{note.id}" />
  <button type="submit">Delete</button>
</form>

<ul>
  {#each notes as note}
    <li>
      {note.title} - {note.content}
      <form method="POST" on:submit={deleteNote}>
        <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{note.id}" />
        <button type="submit">Delete</button>
      </form>
    </li>
  {/each}
</ul>
```

### 3. Check Server-Side Logic

Ensure that your server-side logic (e.g., `/api/notes/{id}` endpoint) is correctly handling the DELETE request and removing the note from the database.

#### Example Server-Side Endpoint (Node.js + Express)

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;

let notes = [
  { id: 1, title: 'Note 1', content: 'Content 1' },
  { id: 2, title: 'Note 2', content: 'Content 2' }
];

app.use(express.json());

// GET all notes
app.get('/api/notes', (req, res) => {
  res.json(notes);
});

// DELETE a note
app.delete('/api/notes/:id', (req, res) => {
  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);
  notes = notes.filter(note => note.id !== id);
  res.status(204).send();
});

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${port}`);
});
```

By following these steps, you should be able to ensure that the UI updates correctly after deleting a note. Make sure to test thoroughly to confirm that everything works as expected.

