    'max_coroutine' => 5,
]);

function doWork($id) {
    echo "Coroutine $id started\n";
    sleep(2);
    echo "Coroutine $id finished\n";
}

for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {
    go(function () use ($i) {
        doWork($i);
    });
}

echo "All coroutines have been created.\n";

?>
```

3. Run the script using the following command:

```bash
php -d extension=swoole.so your_script.php
```

This will create 10 coroutines, but only 5 will run concurrently due to the `max_coroutine` setting. The remaining coroutines will wait until one of the running ones finishes before starting.

Keep in mind that this approach works well when dealing with I/O-bound tasks. If you're working with CPU-bound tasks, consider using other concurrency models like multi-threading or asynchronous programming with non-blocking I/O operations. In such cases, limiting the number of concurrent coroutines might not be necessary or effective.
