Here's a Java program that calculates the GCD of two positive integers entered by the user:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class GCDCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter the first positive integer: ");
        int num1 = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter the second positive integer: ");
        int num2 = scanner.nextInt();

        if (num1 <= 0 || num2 <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Both numbers must be positive.");
        } else {
            int gcd = calculateGCD(num1, num2);
            System.out.println("The GCD of " + num1 + " and " + num2 + " is: " + gcd);
        }

        scanner.close();
    }

    private static int calculateGCD(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int temp = b;
            b = a % b;
            a = temp;
        }
        return a;
    }
}
```

This program uses the Euclidean algorithm to find the GCD of two numbers. It first checks if both input numbers are positive; if not, it prints an error message. Otherwise, it calls the `calculateGCD` method with the two input numbers and prints the result.

